```

Hmm — "不抛出未捕获异常" — swallowing errors from callbacks and logging to console.error might create console error noise (A7 says 控制台无报错). Better: silently swallow? Or use console.warn? Hmm, app-shell is supposed to handle its own errors. If a callback throws, app-shell failed. Catching and logging console.error would violate "控制台无报错" only if errors occur. I think catching is good to prevent input crashes; but maybe better not to log as error. I'd catch and swallow silently? Swallowing is bad practice but A7 prefers no console errors. Let me catch and rethrow asynchronously? That creates uncaught error — bad.

Alternative: don't wrap callbacks at all — if app-shell handlers are correct, no errors. But defensive wrapping helps A7. I'll catch and use console.warn? Hmm console warnings aren't "报错" per se but could count. I'll catch silently but store last error? Overkill.

I think simplest faithful: invoke directly without try/catch, letting app-shell own error handling ("错误兜底" is app-shell responsibility per §2.6). The input layer's job is just dispatch. But then a throwing handler breaks preventDefault ordering? preventDefault should happen before dispatch, so it's already done. If handler throws, we'd get uncaught exception reported. That's app-shell's bug though.

Hmm, for quality, I'll catch and log via console.error only... Actually let me reconsider A7: "全流程无未捕获异常；存储失败降级；控制台无报错". If everything works, no error. Catching is just a safety net; console.error only fires on bug. I'll catch and swallow with optional console.warn guarded. I'll do: try { fn() } catch (e) { /* 交给装配层兜底；输入层不中断监听 */ }. Silent. That's defensible and no console noise.

Actually swallowing completely hides bugs during integration. But the app-shell has its own try/catch. I'll swallow silently but note in comment. Fine.

